WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:06.174
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Emma. Welcome to Online Newspaper Class, level five.

2
00:00:06.474 --> 00:00:09.478
Hi! I'm Visal. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:09.778 --> 00:00:17.097
Unit five: Scientific Papers. Today you will describe methods and results formally with passive modals.

4
00:00:17.397 --> 00:00:20.270
Here is our lesson map. Let's start!

5
00:00:21.233 --> 00:00:24.455
Part one: words. You will learn eight new words.

6
00:00:25.317 --> 00:00:26.654
Research question.

7
00:00:27.650 --> 00:00:31.786
Research question means the specific question a study sets out to answer.

8
00:00:32.383 --> 00:00:34.743
The research question was clear and narrow.

9
00:00:35.350 --> 00:00:38.989
Your turn. Say: Research question.

10
00:00:41.833 --> 00:00:43.194
Research question.

11
00:00:43.950 --> 00:00:45.566
Data analysis.

12
00:00:46.550 --> 00:00:50.697
Data analysis means the process of examining results to find patterns.

13
00:00:51.283 --> 00:00:53.968
Data analysis revealed a clear trend.

14
00:00:54.583 --> 00:00:57.932
Your turn. Say: Data analysis.

15
00:01:00.767 --> 00:01:02.697
Data analysis.

16
00:01:03.450 --> 00:01:04.590
Control group.

17
00:01:05.583 --> 00:01:09.440
Control group means the group that does not receive the treatment being tested.

18
00:01:10.050 --> 00:01:12.433
The control group received no medicine.

19
00:01:13.017 --> 00:01:15.576
Your turn. Say: Control group.

20
00:01:18.433 --> 00:01:19.667
Control group.

21
00:01:20.417 --> 00:01:22.230
Statistical significance.

22
00:01:23.217 --> 00:01:28.037
Statistical significance means a sign that a result is unlikely to be due to chance.

23
00:01:28.650 --> 00:01:31.764
Statistical significance does not prove importance.

24
00:01:32.350 --> 00:01:35.606
Your turn. Say: Statistical significance.

25
00:01:38.467 --> 00:01:40.246
Statistical significance.

26
00:01:40.983 --> 00:01:41.938
Peer-reviewed.

27
00:01:42.950 --> 00:01:47.028
Peer-reviewed means checked by independent experts before being published.

28
00:01:47.617 --> 00:01:50.081
Only peer-reviewed studies were included.

29
00:01:50.683 --> 00:01:53.162
Your turn. Say: Peer-reviewed.

30
00:01:56.000 --> 00:01:56.850
Peer-reviewed.

31
00:01:57.617 --> 00:01:58.699
Replication.

32
00:01:59.683 --> 00:02:03.633
Replication means repeating a study to see whether the results are the same.

33
00:02:04.250 --> 00:02:07.109
Replication builds confidence in a finding.

34
00:02:07.717 --> 00:02:10.439
Your turn. Say: Replication.

35
00:02:13.300 --> 00:02:14.557
Replication.

36
00:02:15.317 --> 00:02:16.341
Findings.

37
00:02:17.350 --> 00:02:20.987
Findings means the results and discoveries reported by a study.

38
00:02:21.583 --> 00:02:23.420
The findings surprised the team.

39
00:02:24.017 --> 00:02:26.448
Your turn. Say: Findings.

40
00:02:29.300 --> 00:02:30.220
Findings.

41
00:02:30.983 --> 00:02:32.077
Conclusion.

42
00:02:33.083 --> 00:02:36.337
Conclusion means the final judgement based on the evidence.

43
00:02:36.950 --> 00:02:39.147
The conclusion was limited to adults.

44
00:02:39.750 --> 00:02:42.542
Your turn. Say: Conclusion.

45
00:02:45.400 --> 00:02:46.448
Conclusion.

46
00:02:47.233 --> 00:02:51.105
Part two: dialogue. Let's listen to a conversation.

47
00:02:51.950 --> 00:02:58.991
Visal, a paper says 'Samples must be stored at minus twenty' and 'The results could have been affected.' What are these structures?

48
00:03:00.083 --> 00:03:12.180
Passive modals. Modal + be + past participle describes duties or possibilities without naming the person. Samples must be stored. Errors can be avoided.

49
00:03:13.283 --> 00:03:14.459
And for the past?

50
00:03:15.550 --> 00:03:27.714
Use modal + have been + past participle: The results may have been affected by heat. The test should have been repeated. It reports what was possible or necessary earlier.

51
00:03:28.817 --> 00:03:30.863
Why is it popular in science?

52
00:03:31.950 --> 00:03:44.181
It keeps attention on the procedure, not on the researcher. Still, avoid vague agents: say 'were recorded by two observers' if who did it affects trust.

53
00:03:45.333 --> 00:03:46.868
Part three: grammar.

54
00:03:47.717 --> 00:03:52.793
Grammar focus: Passive modals: must be and may have been plus past participle.

55
00:03:53.093 --> 00:03:54.431
Look at the pattern.

56
00:03:55.117 --> 00:03:58.091
Samples must be stored at minus twenty degrees.

57
00:03:58.983 --> 00:04:01.285
The results may have been affected by heat.

58
00:04:02.183 --> 00:04:04.798
The test should have been repeated with a larger sample.

59
00:04:05.733 --> 00:04:15.793
Use modal + be + past participle for duties and possibilities, and modal + have been + past participle for the past. Name the agent when it affects trust.

60
00:04:16.550 --> 00:04:20.375
Be careful! Don't say: The data should been checked twice.

61
00:04:22.333 --> 00:04:24.809
Say: The data should have been checked twice.

62
00:04:25.109 --> 00:04:27.097
Add have before been after a modal.

63
00:04:27.900 --> 00:04:30.622
Part four: reading. Read and listen.

64
00:04:31.433 --> 00:04:32.818
Inside a Study.

65
00:04:33.118 --> 00:04:37.137
Every scientific paper begins with a research question that can be tested.

66
00:04:38.133 --> 00:04:43.279
The control group must be treated in exactly the same way, except for the factor being studied.

67
00:04:44.267 --> 00:04:51.491
After data analysis, results can be described as statistically significant only if chance is unlikely to explain them.

68
00:04:52.500 --> 00:04:58.389
A peer-reviewed paper should have been checked, but replication gives even stronger confidence in the findings.

69
00:04:59.400 --> 00:05:02.990
A fair conclusion must be limited to what the evidence can support.

70
00:05:04.100 --> 00:05:08.102
Part five: quiz. Five questions. Are you ready?

71
00:05:08.950 --> 00:05:11.170
The samples blank stored in the cold.

72
00:05:11.470 --> 00:05:15.031
A, must be. B, must. C, must have.

73
00:05:18.567 --> 00:05:20.485
The answer is A: must be.

74
00:05:21.650 --> 00:05:24.671
The group that does not receive the treatment is the blank group.

75
00:05:24.971 --> 00:05:28.601
A, control. B, findings. C, peer.

76
00:05:32.167 --> 00:05:33.911
The answer is A: control.

77
00:05:35.050 --> 00:05:36.701
Which sentence is correct?

78
00:05:37.001 --> 00:05:43.313
A, The test should have repeated.. B, The test should have been repeated.. C, The test should be have repeated..

79
00:05:46.867 --> 00:05:50.016
The answer is B: The test should have been repeated..

80
00:05:51.150 --> 00:05:53.695
Repeating a study to check the results is blank.

81
00:05:53.995 --> 00:05:58.101
A, analysis. B, abstraction. C, replication.

82
00:06:01.667 --> 00:06:03.945
The answer is C: replication.

83
00:06:05.083 --> 00:06:08.964
According to the reading, a fair conclusion must be limited to…

84
00:06:09.264 --> 00:06:14.426
A, the author's opinion. B, what the evidence can support. C, the title.

85
00:06:17.967 --> 00:06:20.814
The answer is B: what the evidence can support.

86
00:06:22.000 --> 00:06:25.337
Part six: speaking. It's your turn to talk.

87
00:06:26.183 --> 00:06:29.843
Why is replication important for trusting scientific findings?

88
00:06:38.650 --> 00:06:40.092
Now listen to Visal.

89
00:06:40.392 --> 00:07:05.759
Replication is important because one study can be affected by chance, error or bias. If other researchers repeat the method and obtain the same results, the findings become far more reliable. If they cannot, the original result may have been a mistake. Replication therefore protects science from weak claims and helps knowledge grow on a solid base.

90
00:07:06.059 --> 00:07:07.710
Excellent answer, Visal!

91
00:07:08.617 --> 00:07:15.332
Great work! Now you can use eight new words and passive modals: must be and may have been plus past participle.

92
00:07:15.632 --> 00:07:21.559
Today's value is scientific literacy. Understand how evidence and testing build knowledge.

93
00:07:21.859 --> 00:07:24.325
See you in Unit six. Goodbye!

94
00:07:24.625 --> 00:07:28.439
Thank you, Ms. Emma! Bye, everyone!

